title: The list commands filter client-side; the API is never asked to filter or paginate
updated: 2026-10-25T05:50:07Z
---

`ardb items`, `ardb quests` and `ardb enemies` fetch the complete collection and filter in Perl:

    my $items = $app->api->items;
    if ($self->search) { $items = [ grep { index(lc($_->name), $search) >= 0 } @$items ]; }
    if ($self->type)   { $items = [ grep { $_->type && lc($_->type) eq $type } @$items ]; }
    if ($self->rarity) { $items = [ grep { $_->rarity && lc($_->rarity) eq $rarity } @$items ]; }

Three consequences:
1. Every invocation downloads and decodes the full collection even when the caller wants three rows.
2. The whole collection lands in the cache file, so the disk cache grows with the full dataset per endpoint.
3. The API's own filtering and pagination are unused — `WWW::ARDB::Request` passes query parameters through, so the plumbing exists.

What to do:
1. Find out what the ardb.app API actually supports for filtering and pagination (see https://ardb.app/developers/api). Only implement what the API really offers — no speculative parameters.
2. If the API filters server-side, add the option to the list command classes (a new MooX::Options option per supported filter) and let the request layer send it.
3. Keep the current client-side behaviour as the fallback when the server does not support the filter, so no existing invocation changes its result.
4. Write tests that pin the new behaviour: with a mock user agent, assert that the request carries the expected query parameter and that the response is used as it comes back — and that the old client-side path still returns the same items when the server ignores the parameter.
5. A filter that moves to the server changes what a caller sees; it needs a `Changes` entry.

If the API turns out to support no filtering at all, do not build a mechanism for it — report that on the card and close it with the finding.

